## Solution

We want the inverse Z-transform of $X(z) = \log\!\left(\dfrac{z}{z-a}\right)$ and the value of $x[0]$.

### Step 1: Rewrite using a convergent series

$$X(z) = \log\!\left(\frac{z}{z-a}\right) = -\log\!\left(1 - \frac{a}{z}\right).$$

For $|a/z| < 1$ (i.e., $|z| > |a|$), use the Mercator series $\log(1-u) = -\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{u^n}{n}$:

$$X(z) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n}\left(\frac{a}{z}\right)^n = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{a^n}{n}\, z^{-n}.$$

### Step 2: Match to the Z-transform definition

The (causal) Z-transform is $X(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} x[n]\, z^{-n}$. Comparing term-by-term:

$$x[n]\, z^{-n} = \frac{a^n}{n}\, z^{-n} \quad\text{for } n \ge 1,$$

and there is **no** $z^{0}$ term in the series (it starts at $n=1$). Therefore:

$$x[n] = \begin{cases} 0, & n = 0,\\[4pt] \dfrac{a^n}{n}, & n \ge 1. \end{cases}$$

### Step 3: Verification via the differentiation property

Differentiate $X(z)$:

$$\frac{dX}{dz} = \frac{1}{z} - \frac{1}{z-a} = \frac{-a}{z(z-a)} = -\frac{a\,z^{-2}}{1-a/z} = -\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a^{k+1}\, z^{-(k+2)}.$$

On the other hand, $\frac{dX}{dz} = -\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n\, x[n]\, z^{-(n+1)}$. Setting $n+1 = k+2$:

$$n\, x[n] = a^n \quad (n \ge 1) \;\Longrightarrow\; x[n] = \frac{a^n}{n},\; n\ge 1,$$

confirming $x[0] = 0$.

### Result

$$\boxed{x[0] = 0}$$

